{
    "title": "Special Supplemental Nutrition Program for Women, Infants, and Children (WIC): Implementation of the Access to Baby Formula Act of 2022 and Related Provisions; Correcting Amendments",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 17, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-17/pdf/2025-23089.pdf",
    "summary": "The government issued a set of fixes to previously updated rules for the nutrition program for women, infants, and children so the Access to Baby Formula Act of 2022 is implemented as intended. These are corrections to missing text and wording errors, and the agency does not expect any changes to how the program operates day to day. The same notice also announces a temporary safety zone on the water for San Francisco\u2019s New Year\u2019s Eve fireworks.\n\nFor the nutrition program, the corrections restore bidding details that were accidentally left out of the infant formula contracting section and clean up language around product recalls, supply chain disruptions, and how benefits are used at the checkout. The updates also standardize wording in state emergency plans, fix minor capitalization and phrasing, and align who approves certain waivers. In plain terms, this is about making the rulebook consistent and complete, not changing benefits or eligibility.\n\nWho benefits? Families who rely on the program should see clearer communication if there\u2019s a recall or shortage, with fewer chances for confusion. State and local agencies, retailers, and formula manufacturers get more precise instructions, which should make contracts and emergency planning smoother. The main downside is minor: agencies and vendors may need to update documents, training materials, or software to match the corrected wording.\n\nSeparately, the Coast Guard will enforce a safety zone near the San Francisco Ferry Building from noon on December 31 through 12:45 a.m. on January 1 to support the city\u2019s fireworks show. This is meant to protect people and the environment while the fireworks barge is loaded, moved, and used. Spectators and crews benefit from a safer event. The inconvenience falls on recreational boaters and nearby water-based businesses that will face temporary access limits and brief delays.",
